    "result_text": "The full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented rejection of a future `registrationDate` on owner creation, returning **400 Bad Request**, following the repo's existing `PostcodeValidator` / `InvalidPostcodeException` convention:\n\n1. **`util/InvalidRegistrationDateException.java`** (new) \u2014 a `RuntimeException` thrown when the supplied registration date lies in the future, mirroring `InvalidPostcodeException`.\n\n2. **`util/RegistrationDateValidator.java`** (new) \u2014 validates a supplied date, accepting `null` (which still defaults to the server date on persist via `Owner.onCreate()`) and throwing when `registrationDate.isAfter(LocalDate.now())`.\n\n3. **`rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 in `addOwner`, calls `RegistrationDateValidator.validate(owner.getRegistrationDate())` right after the existing `PostcodeValidator.validate(...)`.\n\n4. **`rest/advice/ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`** \u2014 added an `@ExceptionHandler(InvalidRegistrationDateException.class)` that maps the exception to a 400 `ProblemDetail`, consistent with the telephone/postcode handlers.\n\nThe acceptance test `errorRejectsFutureDate` (posting `registrationDate: \"2999-01-01\"`) now gets a 400, and the validator leaves the common case (omitted/past date) untouched.",
